      "abstract": "Abstract Georg Luk\u00e1cs\u2019s History and Class Consciousness (1923) has often been criticised for its idealism. I discuss Luk\u00e1cs\u2019s critique of reification in light of these charges, identifying two different idealist vocabularies of (neo-)Kantian and Hegelian origin, respectively. I show that the function of the former is critical: refracting Marx\u2019s analysis of social form through a Kantian form/content distinction allows Luk\u00e1cs to critique capitalism as the domination of form over content. Luk\u00e1cs\u2019s Hegelian leanings are more problematic, however, as they constrain his own insights into the conditioned and contingent character of political practice and history.",
